(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-data-validity-variation-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Data, Validity and Variation Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does a frequency distribution portray?","choices":["The average value across the sample","The spread of values around the mean","The quantity in each category, class or interval","The proportion of people who develop a condition"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, showing the number or proportion of people with different values. It can be used on nominal variables as well as interval data. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What does reliability mean?","choices":["The measurement captures what it is meant to","The result applies to other populations","Repeated attempts produce similar results","The measurement is free of systematic error"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it goes by several other names: reproducibility, repeatability and precision. Note that none of them implies the result is correct. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does systematic variation do to results?","choices":["It balances out over many measurements","It can be removed by taking an average","It affects only individual measurements","It biases them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and unlike random variation it does not balance out with more measurements, so averaging does not help. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is criterion validity?","choices":["The measure covers all relevant dimensions of the concept","The measure predicts or associates with an observable event","The measure agrees with related measures of the same thing","The measure gives consistent results on repetition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as in whether chronically high cholesterol is accompanied by xanthelasma, which is an outside observation the measure can be checked against. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What does a measurement with low validity and high reliability look like?","choices":["Tightly clustered results, all away from the true value","Widely scattered results centred on the true value","Tightly clustered results on the true value","Widely scattered results away from the true value"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is the most dangerous combination, because the consistency is easily mistaken for correctness when nothing is checked against a known standard. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How can the validity of a measuring device be ensured?","choices":["By repeating the measurement several times","By calibrating it against known values","By increasing the sample size","By blinding the observer to the result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and where the thing measured is more subjective, structured questions serve the same purpose, as with alcohol screening questionnaires and pain scales. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"One clinician grades a murmur as three and another grades it as four. Which variation is that?","choices":["Intraobserver variation","Interobserver variation","Intrasubject variation","Systematic measurement error"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because two different observers reached different readings of the same finding rather than one observer differing from themselves. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is dichotomous data?","choices":["Ordinal data with exactly two ranks","Interval data rounded to two values","Nominal data divided into two categories","Data with two possible measurement scales"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with yes and no, or present and absent, as the examples. It is a special case of nominal data rather than a category of its own. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What helps establish validity for more subjective measurements?","choices":["Structured questions","Calibration against a physical standard","Taking the average of several observers","Repeating the measurement on another day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with alcohol screening questionnaires and pain scales as the examples, since neither quantity can be checked against a physical standard. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is content validity?","choices":["The measure predicts an observable event","The measure covers all relevant dimensions and excludes irrelevant ones","The measure agrees with others of the same phenomenon","The measure gives the same result on repetition"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the exclusion half matters as much as the inclusion: a depression scale that also captured unrelated distress would fail on the same criterion. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What defines interval data?","choices":["Order without defined intervals","Categories with no order","Order with defined, equal intervals","Values restricted to two categories"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it splits into two subtypes: continuous data, which can take any value in a range, and discrete data, which takes only specific values. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What stages are named in the progression toward death?","choices":["Susceptibility, pre-symptomatic, clinical disease, dysfunction","Exposure, incubation, infection, recovery","Nominal, ordinal, interval, continuous","Endemic, epidemic, pandemic, eradication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and setting them out as stages is what makes the point: there is no single moment at which a person crosses from well to ill. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What produces true biologic variation?","choices":["A single known factor affecting the measurement","A flaw in the measuring process","Disagreement between two observers","Many unknown factors each contributing a small random effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why consecutive systolic pressures taken under the same conditions will not be exactly the same in the same patient. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What is the mode?","choices":["The middle value of the ordered data","The sum divided by the number of observations","The value two standard deviations above the mean","The most frequently occurring value"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is the only one of the three that need not be a value near the centre at all, since a distribution can have its peak anywhere. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the weakness of the mean?","choices":["It cannot be calculated for interval data","It can be affected by extreme outliers","It ignores the number of observations","It is the same as the mode in all distributions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why the median, defined by position rather than magnitude, holds up better when a few values sit far from the rest. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What distinguishes continuous from discrete data?","choices":["Continuous takes only specific values","Continuous can take any value in a range","Discrete data has no defined intervals","Discrete data has no order"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, though limited by the precision of the measurement, whereas discrete data takes only specific values such as a number of children. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Why should an abnormal test be repeated?","choices":["Because the laboratory may have lost the sample","Because repeated testing improves validity","Because of regression to the mean","Because the reference range changes over time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: an extreme value measured once tends to sit closer to the average when measured again, so the first result may overstate the departure. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What does validity mean in measurement?","choices":["Repeated measurements give similar results","The results apply to other clinical settings","The measurement is free of random variation","The data measure what they are supposed to measure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is also called accuracy. It can be ensured by calibrating devices and by comparing results when measuring known values. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the normal distribution based on?","choices":["Measurements taken from healthy populations","The distribution of disease in a community","The precision of the measuring instrument","Mathematical theory rather than actual measurement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that is worth remembering before treating a reference range as a fact about bodies: the curve is a model that real data may or may not follow. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is intraobserver variation?","choices":["One observer interpreting the same thing differently twice","Two observers interpreting the same thing differently","One patient's value changing between measurements","A flaw in the measuring instrument"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with a radiologist reviewing the same radiograph at two different times and reaching different interpretations as the example. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What does the standard deviation describe?","choices":["The variability of scores about the mean","The full spread from lowest to highest value","The middle value of the distribution","The most frequently occurring value"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is the measure the normal distribution is expressed in: about two thirds of values within one standard deviation, and 95 per cent within two. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"In what context should abnormality be determined?","choices":["Whatever falls outside two standard deviations","Values known to be associated with disease, disability or death","Whatever the laboratory reports as high or low","Whatever differs from the patient's previous result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, together with a clinically recognisable difference from healthy, rather than by statistical position alone. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What two phenomena combine to produce fluctuation in clinical measurements?","choices":["Bias and publication practice","Sampling and randomisation","Biologic differences and variation in measurement","Prevalence and incidence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and separating them matters: one describes the patient genuinely differing, the other the process of measuring failing to pin the value down. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What three sources can explain variation within one patient?","choices":["Bias, chance and confounding","Sampling, blinding and randomisation","Biologic variation, measurement error, and real disease change","Incidence, prevalence and case fatality"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and identifying which is operating is the clinical point: intervention should only be considered if there is a true pathological change. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What does the range describe?","choices":["All values from the lowest to the highest","The values between the 25th and 75th percentiles","The variability of scores about the mean","The middle of the distribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which makes it wholly dependent on the two most extreme observations, and is why the interquartile range exists as a limited alternative. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What characterises ordinal data?","choices":["Categories with no order at all","Equal defined intervals between values","Only two possible values","Order among categories without defined intervals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: one value can be seen as more, less or equally desirable than another, but the distance between adjacent categories is not defined or equal. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the interquartile range?","choices":["The full spread from lowest to highest value","The variability of scores about the mean","The proportion of values within one standard deviation","A limited range index, such as the 25th to 75th percentiles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and limiting it that way is the point: it describes the spread of the bulk of the data without being dictated by the two extreme values. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What limits how finely continuous data can be recorded?","choices":["The number of categories chosen in advance","The precision of the measurement","The size of the sample","The type of distribution the data follow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The underlying quantity varies continuously, but what can be recorded is bounded by the instrument and the method used to read it. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does systematic measurement error determine?","choices":["A range for the true score","The reliability of the instrument","The prevalence of the condition","The central tendency of the distribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and that is the practical consequence of a known flaw in the process: the reading is not the value, but it bounds where the value lies. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What is intrasubject variation?","choices":["Variation within one person across measurements","Variation between two observers reading the same finding","Variation in one observer reading the same thing twice","Variation between different patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, as with blood pressure varying by biological variation, time of day, position and stress, all in the same patient. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"}]};
